## Summary
NASA initiated the NASA Art Program in 1962 under administrator James Webb, enlisting over 350 artists including Andy Warhol, Annie Leibovitz, and Norman Rockwell to visually document America's space program. The program allowed artists creative freedom without censorship, aiming to capture space exploration through artistic perspectives.

## Key Points
- The NASA Art Program was created in 1962 by NASA administrator James Webb.
- It involved over 350 artists, including renowned figures like Andy Warhol, Annie Leibovitz, and Norman Rockwell.
- Artists were not edited or censored, ensuring free artistic expression.
- The program was motivated by the popularity of space-related imagery to document space exploration visually.


